WEBVTT

00:03.840 --> 00:04.640
All right, friends.

00:04.640 --> 00:07.440
So now let's have a quick recap about the SQL window functions.

00:07.440 --> 00:08.840
Let's start with the definition.

00:08.880 --> 00:14.840
It's going to go and perform calculations like aggregations on top of subsets of data without losing

00:14.840 --> 00:16.240
the level of details.

00:16.240 --> 00:18.600
So that means we can do aggregations.

00:18.600 --> 00:21.400
And at the same time we are not losing the details.

00:21.440 --> 00:25.520
Now, of course, there is a lot of similarity between the window function and the groupby, but the

00:25.520 --> 00:31.600
main differences are that window functions are very powerful and dynamic compared to the groupby.

00:31.640 --> 00:34.240
We have way more functions than the groupby, right?

00:34.240 --> 00:39.920
But now if you are doing data analysis and you have an advanced use case, then you have to go and use

00:39.920 --> 00:40.760
window function.

00:40.760 --> 00:44.240
It's more suitable for complex and advanced data analysis.

00:44.400 --> 00:49.720
But in the other hand, if you have a simple question, simple data analysis, then you can go and use

00:49.720 --> 00:52.280
the aggregate functions using the groupby.

00:52.320 --> 00:56.320
And of course you can go and use them in the same query, in the same select.

00:56.360 --> 00:59.800
You can go and mix the groupby together with the window function.

00:59.800 --> 01:03.120
With only one rule you have to use the same columns.

01:03.160 --> 01:08.040
And of course the first step is to do the groupby, and then later you do the window function in the

01:08.080 --> 01:08.760
same query.

01:08.800 --> 01:11.640
And now to the next point about the window components.

01:11.640 --> 01:13.150
We have two main components.

01:13.150 --> 01:18.070
The first one is the window function and the second part is the window definition.

01:18.070 --> 01:22.310
Using the over clause and inside the over clause, we can define three things.

01:22.310 --> 01:28.270
If you want to divide the data to create windows, you can use the partition by the second section.

01:28.270 --> 01:31.510
We have the order by in order to sort your data.

01:31.510 --> 01:37.110
And the last part you can go and specify a subset of data like a frame within each window.

01:37.110 --> 01:38.430
Now let's move to the last part.

01:38.470 --> 01:40.990
We have rules for the SQL window functions.

01:40.990 --> 01:45.910
So the first thing is that if you have two window functions or multiple window functions, you cannot

01:45.910 --> 01:47.710
go and nest them together.

01:47.710 --> 01:50.830
You have to go and use multiple subqueries.

01:50.830 --> 01:56.950
The next point is that you can use the window function only in the select and the order by clause.

01:56.950 --> 02:02.110
So for example, you cannot use the window together with the Where clause in order to filter the data.

02:02.150 --> 02:06.470
Talking about filtering data, how SQL is going to go and execute the window function.

02:06.470 --> 02:09.550
It's always after SQL filter the data.

02:09.590 --> 02:09.910
All right.

02:09.910 --> 02:12.630
So those are the basic stuff about the SQL window function.

02:12.630 --> 02:16.390
So with that you have learned the basics about the window functions in SQL.

02:16.430 --> 02:18.550
And next we're going to start talking about the functions.

02:18.550 --> 02:21.150
So the first group is the window aggregate functions.

02:21.150 --> 02:25.270
And here we're going to learn how to summarize our data for a specific group of rows.

02:25.270 --> 02:25.950
So let's go.
